im interface <interface-name> mode sparse\\n##\\n\\nFor the exact translation of the Cisco configuration, you would need to specify each interface separately in Juniper, like:\\n##\\nset protocols pim interface ge-1/0/0 mode sparse\\nset protocols pim interface ge-1/0/1 mode sparse\\nset protocols pim interface ge-1/0/2 mode sparse\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set protocols pim interface ge-1/0/2 mode sparse'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 pim sparse-mode', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 pim sparse-mode` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse\\n##\\n\\nNote: You would replace `<interface-name>` with the actual Juniper interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0`) when applying this configuration. The command enables PIM sparse mode for IPv6 on the specified interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse'}]]": 1,
